  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Compute $P(X>4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(4<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}]}_{4}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
